abstract: >-
  The first implementation of the Duan–Mao–Shu–Yin directed single-source
  shortest-path algorithm (arXiv:2602.07868, February 2026): a Python
  reference, a Rust port gated bit-exactly against it, and a variant study
  with all distances verified against Dijkstra. Measured: the algorithm is
  not faster at any storable input size. The faithful port runs at 38–182x
  of a production Dijkstra, the most engineered variant at 1.8–2.2x, and
  the prescribed degree parameter falls outside the paper's own validity
  range on every input below n = 2^4096. Audited: the distances are exact
  on every input run, but the printed pseudocode crashes on ordinary graphs
  at two lines, and the completeness clause of Lemma 3.7 is false as
  stated. A 70-vertex counterexample settles an incomplete vertex, and a
  variant that trusts the clause finishes with a wrong distance. The
  repository also includes a hand proof, derived and independently
  re-derived across thirty-two recorded passes, that a capped variant of
  the algorithm settles every vertex with its canonical (length, hop count)
  label.
